# Loom

Loom is a literate programming framework. You write a document — prose and code together, in the order that reads best — and Loom tangles it, composing the code into real source files. The prose is not documentation wrapped around the code but part of the program: the layer a person reads to learn what the code does and why.

This book is written in Loom, in the framework's own source, and it is the framework's only specification: the design notes that once sat apart from the code now live here, in the chapters that describe it, where they cannot drift. `loom weave` renders the book as the website you are reading — every chapter a page, its prose and its code side by side. Read it from the front. It opens on how the book is laid out and read, then opens the machine, each part building on the one before. To use Loom rather than read how it works, the docs are the place to start.

